.TH STRING 3
.SH NAME
strcat, strncat, strcmp, strncmp, strcpy, strncpy, strlen, strchr, strrchr, strpbrk, strspn, strcspn, strtok, strdup, index, rindex \- string operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <string.h>

char *strcat(s1, s2)		char *strncat(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;			char *s1, *s2; int n;

int strcmp(s1, s2)		int strncmp(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;			char *s1, *s2; int n;

char *strcpy(s1, s2)		char *strncpy(s1, s2, n)
char *s1, *s2;			char *s1, *s2; int n;

int strlen(s)
char *s;

char *strchr(s, c)		char *strrchr(s, c)
char *s; int c;			char *s; int c;

char *index(s, c)		char *rindex(s, c)
char *s; int c;			char *s; int c;

char *strpbrk(s1, s2)
char *s1, *s2;

int strspn(s1, s2)		int strcspn(s1, s2)
char *s1, *s2;			char *s1, *s2;

char *strtok(s1, s2)
char *s1, *s2;

char *strdup(s)
char *s;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A string is an array of characters ended by a NUL, a zero byte. These
functions work on strings, and do not check for overflow of the arrays
they write into.
.TP 10
.B strcat
appends a copy of
.I s2
to the end of
.IR s1 .
.B strncat
appends at most
.I n
characters of
.IR s2 ,
and then a NUL. Both return
.IR s1 .
.TP
.B strcmp
compares
.I s1
and
.I s2
and returns a number less than, equal to, or greater than 0, as
.I s1
comes before
.IR s2 ,
is the same, or comes after it, in the order of the ASCII character
set: the characters are compared as unsigned numbers.
.B strncmp
compares at most
.I n
characters.
.TP
.B strcpy
copies
.I s2
into
.IR s1 ,
up to and including its NUL.
.B strncpy
copies exactly
.I n
characters: if
.I s2
is shorter, the rest of the
.I n
are NULs; if it is
.I n
characters or longer, only the first
.I n
are copied, with no NUL after them. Both return
.IR s1 .
.TP
.B strlen
returns the number of characters in
.IR s ,
not counting the NUL.
.TP
.B strchr
returns a pointer to the first occurrence of the character
.I c
in
.IR s ,
and
.B strrchr
to the last, or NULL if
.I c
does not occur in it. The NUL that ends the string can be found too:
strchr(s, 0) points to it.
.B index
and
.B rindex
are the same as strchr and strrchr.
.TP
.B strpbrk
returns a pointer to the first character in
.I s1
that is one of the characters in
.IR s2 ,
or NULL if there is none.
.TP
.B strspn
returns the length of the longest initial part of
.I s1
made only of characters in
.IR s2 ,
and
.B strcspn
of the longest made only of characters not in it.
.TP
.B strtok
splits the string
.I s1
into tokens: strings of characters not in
.IR s2 ,
separated by one or more characters that are. The first call, with
.IR s1 ,
returns a pointer to the first token; each later call, with NULL as its
first argument, returns the next token, and NULL when there are none
left.
.I s2
may differ from one call to the next. strtok writes a NUL into
.I s1
at the end of each token it returns, and remembers where it stopped, so
only one string can be split at a time.
.TP
.B strdup
returns a pointer to a copy of
.IR s ,
in memory from
.BR malloc (3),
or NULL if no memory is left.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	char line[] = "cc \-O \-o prog prog.c";
	char *t;

	t = strtok(line, " ");
	while (t != NULL) {
		printf("%s\en", t);
		t = strtok((char *)0, " ");
	}
.fi
.PP
prints each word of the line on a line of its own.
.SH SEE ALSO
memory(3), malloc(3), ctype(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR strcat ,
.B strcmp
and
.B strcpy
functions first appeared outside of Bell Laboratories in PWB/UNIX 1.0.
.B strlen
first appeared in Version 6 AT&T UNIX;
.BR strncat ,
.BR strncmp ,
.BR strncpy ,
.B index
and
.B rindex
in Version 7;
.BR strchr ,
.BR strrchr ,
.BR strpbrk ,
.BR strspn ,
.B strcspn
and
.B strtok
in System III.
.PP
.B strdup
is not in the C libraries of Version 7 AT&T UNIX or 4.3BSD.
.SH BUGS
strcmp and strncmp compare by the numeric values of the characters,
which is alphabetical order only for letters of the same case.
